    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/srep02245",
    "abstract": "Abstract In the late twentieth century, the sub-thermocline waters of the southern tropical and subtropical Indian Ocean experienced a sharp cooling. This cooling has been previously attributed to an anthropogenic aerosol-induced strengthening of the global ocean conveyor, which transfers heat from the subtropical gyre latitudes toward the North Atlantic. From the mid-1990s the sub-thermocline southern Indian Ocean experienced a rapid temperature trend reversal. Here we show, using climate models from phase 5 of the Coupled Model Intercomparison Project, that the late twentieth century sub-thermocline cooling of the southern Indian Ocean was primarily driven by increasing anthropogenic aerosols and greenhouse gases. The models simulate a slow-down in the sub-thermocline cooling followed by a rapid warming towards the mid twenty-first century. The simulated evolution of the Indian Ocean temperature trend is linked with the peak in aerosols and their subsequent decline in the twenty-first century, reinforcing the hypothesis that aerosols influence ocean circulation trends.",
    "field": "Climate change attribution",
    "problem": "The cause of the observed late-20th-century cooling and subsequent rapid warming in the sub-thermocline southern Indian Ocean is actively debated, challenging efforts to attribute these trends to either natural variability or specific anthropogenic forcings like aerosols and greenhouse gases.",
    "method": "This study analyzes CMIP5 climate model simulations, comparing experiments with all historical forcings against those with individual forcings like aerosols and greenhouse gases, to attribute the cause of observed sub-thermocline temperature trends in the southern Indian Ocean.",
    "task": "The task is to use climate models to determine the role of anthropogenic aerosols in driving the observed late twentieth-century cooling and subsequent temperature trend reversal in the sub-thermocline waters of the southern Indian Ocean.",
