            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Asia",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "Anthropogenic forcings, such as GHGs have already been attributed as the major cause of historic ocean warming\u00b9\u2079. Aside from GHGs, anthropogenic aerosols (AAs) have been implicated in forcing changes in the large-scale climate, such as the south Asian and East Asian monsoon\u00b2\u2070\u207b\u00b2\u00b2 and North Atlantic sea surface temperatures (SSTs)\u00b2\u00b3, including cross-hemispheric changes in the tropical atmospheric circulation\u00b2\u2074\u207b\u00b2\u2076.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "tropical cyclones",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Arabian Sea",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "natural aerosols (e.g., sea salt, dust) are also thought to have forced changes to the surface radiation balance and temperatures in the Arabian Sea\u00b2\u2077, leading to a late twentieth century increase in pre-monsoonal tropical cyclones\u00b2\u2078.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Specifically, model simulations of an AA-induced response show a strengthening in the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation and an increase in the cross-hemispheric transport of heat from the SH to the Northern Hemisphere (NH) via the global conveyor\u00b2\u2079.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Deep Water formation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "As a result, heat is removed from the subtropical latitudes of the southern IO sub-thermocline, and is transported to the North Atlantic, increasing the stability of North Atlantic Deep Water formation\u00b2.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Pacific Decadal Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Indian Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Toward the end of the twentieth century the subsurface tropical IO exhibited a rapid warming\u00b9\u00b2, which coincided with a shift to the negative phase of the Pacific Decadal Oscillation (PDO).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Pacific Decadal Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Pacific Ocean",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The switch to a positive PDO phase in the late 1970s aligns with the sharp decline in IO temperatures during this period (Fig. 3b), however the lack of a warming prior to the 1980s (negative PDO phase) suggests that external forcings may have also played a role. In the Pacific, a rapid warming occurs in the late 1980s and then temperatures dip again before the end of the twentieth century, evidence of strong multi-decadal variability (Fig. S4c).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "expendable bathythermographs",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MEASURES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "temperature",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "The trend cross sections in Fig. 1 are based on three different observational datasets: (i) Levitus09 temperature from the World Ocean Database 2009\u00b9,\u2075\u2075, which is based on Argo profiles, expendable bathythermographs (XBT), and mechanical bathythermographs (MBT); (ii) Ishii09 temperature\u2075\u2076, with bias corrected XBT and MBT data; and (iii) Simple Ocean Data Assimilation (SODA)- Parallel Ocean Program (POP) version 2.2.4\u2075\u2077, which is an assimilated product that is forced using 20th Century Reanalysis V2 winds.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "mechanical bathythermographs",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MEASURES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "temperature",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "The trend cross sections in Fig. 1 are based on three different observational datasets: (i) Levitus09 temperature from the World Ocean Database 2009\u00b9,\u2075\u2075, which is based on Argo profiles, expendable bathythermographs (XBT), and mechanical bathythermographs (MBT); (ii) Ishii09 temperature\u2075\u2076, with bias corrected XBT and MBT data; and (iii) Simple Ocean Data Assimilation (SODA)- Parallel Ocean Program (POP) version 2.2.4\u2075\u2077, which is an assimilated product that is forced using 20th Century Reanalysis V2 winds.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Argo floats",
            "source_entity_type": "Instrument",
            "relation": "MEASURES_VARIABLE",
            "target_entity_instance": "temperature",
            "target_entity_type": "Variable",
            "paragraph": "For Fig. 3, Levitus09, Ishii09, and SODA-POP are used, as well as annual values from Argo floats (2005-2012) interpolated onto a 1\u00b0 \u00d7 1\u00b0 grid\u2075\u2078. Each observational estimate is also vertically interpolated to the Ishii09 resolution. A PDO index\u2075\u2079, with a 144-month low pass filter is also used to look at multi-decadal relationships.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
